    // The window key list itself. The old hand-written script overrode this and the generated one
    // dropped it, which left Chromium's real global surface -- chrome, Notification, BatteryManager,
    // Bluetooth, USB, PushManager, external and every Blink-only interface -- visible to anything
    // that enumerates. hCaptcha calls Object.keys 339 times in the capture.
    //
    // The device list is a top-level page, and it holds BUILT-INS ONLY. The frame-local names
    // hCaptcha's own iframe carries -- hsw, Raven, _sharedLibs, __wdata -- are not in it and are not
    // appended to it: topList() picks them up from the realm's real enumeration, so they appear only
    // where they exist, at the END, which is the only place a global that script created can be.
    //
    // They used to be appended unconditionally, with `hsw` spliced in immediately before `window`
    // (index 6) on the strength of a reading of the original in-frame capture. That was wrong, and it
    // cost a measurable field: hsw sweeps the LAST 25 window names, so at index 6 `hsw` was never in
    // range and never appeared in the suspicious-globals list -- while the iPhone and an unspoofed
    // Chromium both report it there (unspoofed it sits at index 991). No engine can put a
    // script-created global among the first built-ins; the capture's own injected globals, __gCrWeb
    // and webkit, are the last two entries of this list, which is where document-start injections
    // belong.
    //
    // WINDOW_ENUM used to end with `webkit` as well, and that was wrong: the device says `webkit` is
    // NON-enumerable. The proof is in the device's own payload rather than in the capture. hsw builds
    // its suspicious-globals list in two passes -- first over Object.keys(window).slice(-25), then
    // over getOwnPropertyNames(window).slice(-25) -- and appends, so a name reported from the
    // enumerable pass lands BEFORE the script globals and one reported only from the own-names pass
    // lands after them. The iPhone reported ["__gCrWeb","hsw","__wdata","webkit"], with `webkit`
    // LAST, after two script-created globals. It cannot have been dropped for distance either: both
    // slices take the last 25 and the enumerable list is a subset of the own-names list, so anything
    // close enough to the end to appear in one is close enough in the other. The only arrangement
